<Layout title="What should I do with a large inheritance? | RIPL Wealth" description="You usually don't need to make every decision about an inheritance immediately. First understand exactly what you received, what deadlines or tax considerations…"><section class="hero"><div class="wrap article"><div class="eyebrow">MONEY QUESTIONS</div><h1>What should I do with a large inheritance?</h1><p class="small">By RIPL Wealth | Updated October 2026</p><p class="answer"><strong>You usually don't need to make every decision about an inheritance immediately. First understand exactly what you received, what deadlines or tax considerations need attention and what can safely wait.</strong></p></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap article"><h2>Identify what you inherited before moving it</h2><p>Cash, real estate, taxable securities and retirement accounts bring different decisions. Gather account statements, estate documents and available valuation records. Separate money you may need soon from assets that can support longer-term goals.</p><h2>Retirement accounts need their own review</h2><p>An inherited IRA is not the same as inherited cash. Distribution requirements depend on the beneficiary, account type and circumstances of the original owner. Confirm applicable deadlines with your tax professional before withdrawing or transferring assets.</p><h2>What could change the answer?</h2><ul class="list"><li>What assets did you inherit and how are they titled?</li><li>Are there decisions or deadlines that genuinely require attention now?</li><li>Do you need any of the money in the near term?</li><li>What do you want this inheritance to make possible?</li></ul><h2>Let's make this real.</h2><div class="callout"><p><strong>Hypothetical example. For illustrative purposes only.</strong></p><p>Someone inherits a taxable investment account and an IRA. A single instruction to move everything could overlook important differences. Reviewing ownership, valuation records and IRA deadlines first makes it possible to build a plan for each asset.</p></div><h2>Here's what we’d want to know about you.</h2><ul class="list"><li>What do you want this money to accomplish?</li><li>When might you need it?</li><li>What else do you own?</li><li>What are you most worried about getting wrong?</li><li>If we improved this part of your financial life, what would that allow you to do?</li></ul><p class="answer"><strong>An inheritance can change your financial life. It doesn't require you to change everything about it on day one.</strong></p><div class="related"><strong>Related:</strong> <a href="/money-questions/">Explore Money Questions</a> · <a href="/financial-transitions/">Planning through a financial transition</a></div><a class="btn" href="/contact/">Start the Conversation </a><div class="sources"><h2>Source and further reading</h2><p><a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Publication 590-B: IRA Distributions</a></p></div><div class="author-box"><h3>RIPL Wealth</h3><p>Independent wealth planning team helping successful individuals and business owners make better decisions with the wealth they've already built.</p><a href="/about/">About RIPL </a></div></div></section></Layout>
